Depends: python3, python3-gi, gir1.2-gtk-3.0, gir1.2-ayatanaappindicator3-0.1, gir1.2-gstreamer-1.0, gir1.2-gst-plugins-base-1.0, gstreamer1.0-pipewire, gstreamer1.0-plugins-good, gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad, gstreamer1.0-plugins-ugly, systemd, xvfb, x11vnc, novnc, websockify, openbox, tint2, xterm, x11-utils, x11-xserver-utils, xauth, iproute2, network-manager, avahi-daemon, ca-certificates, polkitd, pkexec, dnsmasq-base, libimobiledevice-utils, usbmuxd, ipheth-utils, adb
Recommends: gnome-shell-extension-appindicator, firefox | chromium | chromium-browser
Homepage: https://github.com/tpluharik/tuxdisplay
Description: use an iPad or Android tablet as a GNOME Wayland monitor over USB
 TuxDisplay creates a real extended monitor or mirrors the primary GNOME
 Wayland screen and streams it directly to a compatible OpenDisplay receiver
 over a normal USB cable. Apple
 devices use usbmuxd and Android devices use an ADB tunnel. No IP network or
 Internet connection is required. An authenticated browser client remains
 available on private networks. Other desktop sessions use an isolated X11
 workspace rather than extending the current desktop.
